(window.CLART=window.CLART||{})["oesoph"]={"h": "<section class=\"arch-c amc\" id=\"a-c1\" data-t=\"Summary\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2>Summary</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Where, what and how fast.</p><p>The shared chapters cover the anatomy, swallowing, and the approach to dysphagia with oesophagoscopy. Each disease then has its own chapter: inflammation and injury (<button type=\"button\" class=\"arx\" data-artgo=\"a-c4\">Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease</button>, <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx\" data-artgo=\"a-c8\">Caustic injury</button>, <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx\" data-artgo=\"a-c9\">Perforation</button>), motility disorders (<button type=\"button\" class=\"arx\" data-artgo=\"a-c10\">Achalasia</button>), structural disorders and tumours (<button type=\"button\" class=\"arx\" data-artgo=\"a-c21\">Oesophageal cancer</button>).</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc\" id=\"a-c2\" data-t=\"Applied anatomy\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2>Applied anatomy</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">A 25-centimetre muscular tube.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s2-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">2.1</span><h3>Course and narrowings</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The oesophagus is about 25 cm long. It runs from the cricopharyngeus at C6 to the cardia at T11, deviating slightly to the left in the neck and again in the lower chest. Along the way it has three normal constrictions, measured at endoscopy from the upper incisors:</p><ol class=\"arol\"><li>The pharyngo-oesophageal junction at C6, about <em class=\"term\">15 cm</em>.</li><li>The crossing of the aortic arch and left main bronchus at T4, about <em class=\"term\">25 cm</em>.</li><li>The diaphragmatic hiatus at T10, about <em class=\"term\">40 cm</em>, with the cardia at about 40 to 42 cm.</li></ol><p>These narrowings are where foreign bodies lodge, where caustic burns are worst and where instruments perforate, so their distances are worth knowing by heart.</p><figure class=\"arfig\" id=\"a-f1\"><div class=\"pair\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-arfig=\"d67-1\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 1\"><img src=\"clinic/fig/d67-1.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-arfig=\"d67-2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 1\"><img src=\"clinic/fig/d67-2.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 1</b>First, the oesophagus and its normal constrictions, with distances from the upper incisors. Second, the oesophagus with its sphincters and the change from striated to smooth muscle.<cite>Dhingra, figs 67.1 and 67.2</cite></figcaption></figure></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s2-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">2.2</span><h3>Wall and supply</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The wall has a mucosa of non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium, a submucosa carrying <em class=\"term\">Meissner's</em> plexus, and inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle with <em class=\"term\">Auerbach's (myenteric)</em> plexus between them. Outside there is only adventitia. The absence of a <em class=\"term\">serosa</em> is the reason leaks spread freely through the mediastinum and anastomoses are fragile.</p><p>The muscle is striated in the upper third, mixed in the middle third and smooth in the lower third, which explains why diseases of skeletal muscle affect the top and scleroderma the bottom. The upper third is supplied by the inferior thyroid artery, the middle by branches of the aorta, and the lower by the left gastric and inferior phrenic arteries. The lower oesophagus is a portosystemic anastomosis and the site of <em class=\"term\">varices</em>. Lymph drains to deep cervical, mediastinal and coeliac nodes, and a long submucosal lymphatic network allows <em class=\"term\">skip lesions</em> in cancer. The nerve supply is from the vagus and the sympathetic chain.</p><figure class=\"arfig arapp\" id=\"a-f2\"><div class=\"pair\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"s227_55\" data-set=\"s227_55,s228_59\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 2\"><img src=\"fig2/t/s227_55.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"s228_59\" data-set=\"s227_55,s228_59\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 2\"><img src=\"fig2/t/s228_59.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 2</b>Constrictions of the oesophagus and their distances from the incisors, and the layers and innervation of the oesophagus.<cite data-figcite=\"s227_55,s228_59\">From the anatomy atlas</cite></figcaption></figure></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s2-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">2.3</span><h3>Sphincters</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The <b>upper oesophageal sphincter</b> is mainly the <em class=\"term\">cricopharyngeus</em>, a zone 3 to 5 cm long that stays tonically closed at rest to stop air entering the oesophagus and refluxate entering the pharynx. On swallowing it relaxes and is pulled open by elevation of the larynx. Above it, between the thyropharyngeus and the cricopharyngeus, lies <em class=\"term\">Killian's dehiscence</em>, the site of the pharyngeal pouch; below it, between the cricopharyngeus and the oesophageal muscle, lies <em class=\"term\">Laimer's triangle</em>.</p><p>The <b>lower oesophageal sphincter</b> is a physiological high-pressure zone rather than an anatomical sphincter. It is helped by the \"pinch-cock\" of the diaphragmatic crura, the acute <em class=\"term\">angle of His</em>, the mucosal folds, and the positive intra-abdominal pressure acting on the abdominal segment.</p><figure class=\"arfig arapp\" id=\"a-f3\"><div class=\"pair\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"s226_54\" data-set=\"s226_54,s199_7\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 3\"><img src=\"fig2/t/s226_54.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"s199_7\" data-set=\"s226_54,s199_7\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 3\"><img src=\"fig2/t/s199_7.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 3</b>Killian and Laimer triangles, and Killian dehiscence with a pharyngeal pouch.<cite data-figcite=\"s226_54,s199_7\">From the anatomy atlas</cite></figcaption></figure></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s2-4\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">2.4</span><h3>Swallowing: the three phases</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b2-4-1\"><h4><i>2.4.1</i>The phases</h4><p>The <b>oral phase</b> is voluntary: food is chewed, mixed with saliva, formed into a bolus and pushed back by the tongue against the palate. It ends when the bolus touches the faucial pillars and triggers the reflex swallow.</p><p>The <b>pharyngeal phase</b> is a reflex lasting under a second, a sequence of protective closures and propulsion. The soft palate rises against <em class=\"term\">Passavant's ridge</em> to close the nasopharynx, and the tongue presses against the palate to close the mouth. Breathing stops and the larynx closes from below upwards, at the true folds, then the false folds, then the arytenoids against the epiglottis. The larynx rises and moves forward under the tongue base, which tips the epiglottis down and <em class=\"term\">pulls the upper sphincter open</em>; laryngeal elevation is the single most important factor in opening it. The constrictors then contract in sequence while the cricopharyngeus relaxes.</p><p>In the <b>oesophageal phase</b>, peristalsis carries the bolus to the stomach in about 3 to 8 seconds, with the lower sphincter relaxing ahead of it.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b2-4-2\"><h4><i>2.4.2</i>Peristalsis</h4><p><b>Primary peristalsis</b> is the wave started by a swallow. <b>Secondary peristalsis</b> is set off locally by distension and clears what the first wave left behind. <b>Tertiary contractions</b> are non-propulsive and uncoordinated; they appear in the elderly (<em class=\"term\">presbyoesophagus</em>) and in spasm.</p><figure class=\"arfig arapp\" id=\"a-f4\"><div class=\"pair\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"s206_20\" data-set=\"s206_20,s198_3\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 4\"><img src=\"fig2/t/s206_20.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"s198_3\" data-set=\"s206_20,s198_3\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 4\"><img src=\"fig2/t/s198_3.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 4</b>Passavant ridge, and the pharyngeal constrictors.<cite data-figcite=\"s206_20,s198_3\">From the anatomy atlas</cite></figcaption></figure></div></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc\" id=\"a-c3\" data-t=\"Diagnosis\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2>Diagnosis</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">The approach to dysphagia.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.1</span><h3>Terms</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p><b>Dysphagia</b> is difficulty swallowing. <b>Odynophagia</b> is painful swallowing and suggests ulceration, inflammation or infection. <b>Globus</b> is a persistent feeling of a lump in the throat, worst between meals, with no true difficulty in swallowing food.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.2</span><h3>Oropharyngeal versus oesophageal</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><caption>Two kinds of dysphagia</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Feature</th><th scope=\"col\">Oropharyngeal (pre-oesophageal)</th><th scope=\"col\">Oesophageal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Complaint</td><td>Difficulty starting a swallow; food sticks in the neck</td><td>Food sticks behind the sternum (though it may be felt in the neck)</td></tr><tr><td>Associated</td><td>Coughing or choking on swallowing, nasal regurgitation, drooling, dysarthria, a wet voice</td><td>Regurgitation, heartburn, chest pain, weight loss</td></tr><tr><td>Typical causes</td><td>Stroke, Parkinson disease, motor neurone disease, myasthenia, myositis, cranial nerve palsies, head and neck cancer and its treatment, pharyngeal pouch, cricopharyngeal dysfunction</td><td>Stricture, cancer, achalasia, spasm, eosinophilic oesophagitis, rings and webs, extrinsic compression</td></tr><tr><td>First test</td><td>Examination, flexible endoscopy, videofluoroscopy or FEES</td><td>Endoscopy (upper GI), barium swallow, manometry</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.3</span><h3>Causes by site</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>It helps to think of the tube from the inside out. <b>In the lumen</b> lies a foreign body or food bolus (<button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-fbfood\">Swallowed foreign bodies</button>). <b>In the wall</b> are inflammation (reflux, infection, caustic, eosinophilic, radiation or pill oesophagitis), strictures, webs and rings, tumours, and motility disorders. <b>Outside the wall</b> are the pharyngeal pouch, goitre and thyroid tumours, cervical <em class=\"term\">osteophytes</em> in diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis, mediastinal nodes and tumours, an enlarged left atrium, aortic aneurysm, and vascular rings causing <em class=\"term\">dysphagia lusoria</em>.</p><figure class=\"arfig\" id=\"a-f5\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-arfig=\"d69-1\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 5\"><img src=\"clinic/fig/d69-1.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 5</b>(A and B) Large cervical osteophytes pressing on the oesophagus on barium swallow.<cite>Dhingra, fig. 69.1</cite></figcaption></figure></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-4\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.4</span><h3>History</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The <b>tempo</b> points to the cause. Sudden dysphagia means a foreign body or bolus, often stuck on an existing stricture or tumour; progression over weeks to months means cancer; intermittent symptoms suggest a ring or spasm; and long-standing dysphagia suggests achalasia or a web.</p><p>The <b>consistency</b> separates mechanical from motor disease. Solids first and liquids later indicate a mechanical obstruction such as a stricture or cancer, whereas liquids as bad as or worse than solids from the start indicate a motility disorder such as achalasia, or a neuromuscular cause.</p><p>Other clues complete the picture. Night-time regurgitation of undigested food with gurgling and halitosis suggests a pharyngeal pouch; heartburn suggests reflux and a peptic stricture; nasal regurgitation suggests palatal weakness; and coughing on swallowing means aspiration. Odynophagia suggests candida or herpes in the immunosuppressed, a pill or a caustic. Weight loss, hoarseness or a neck mass suggests cancer, and recurrent bolus impaction in a young atopic man suggests eosinophilic oesophagitis.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-5\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.5</span><h3>Examination and tests</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Examine the mouth and oropharynx, and look at the larynx and hypopharynx with a flexible endoscope for pooling in the pyriform sinuses and for cord movement; then examine the neck, the cranial nerves and the chest. A blood count and iron studies look for iron deficiency and Plummer–Vinson syndrome.</p><p>The <b>barium swallow</b> shows pouches, strictures, webs, rings, achalasia, spasm and extrinsic compression. <b>Videofluoroscopy</b>, the modified barium swallow, studies the oral and pharyngeal phases with different consistencies and demonstrates aspiration, and <b>flexible endoscopic evaluation of swallowing (FEES)</b> shows residue, penetration and aspiration at the bedside. A chest film, a lateral soft-tissue neck film and CT are used for masses.</p><p><b>Endoscopy</b>, either oesophagogastroduodenoscopy or transnasal oesophagoscopy, sees and biopsies the mucosa, and <em class=\"term\">every new oesophageal dysphagia in an adult needs it.</em> For function, <b>high-resolution manometry</b> is the test for motility disorders, classified by the <em class=\"term\">Chicago classification</em>, and <b>24-hour pH and impedance monitoring</b> quantifies reflux.</p><div class=\"arcases\" data-arcases=\"esophageal-passage-disorders\" data-lead=\"Disorders of the oesophageal passage on imaging.\"></div><div class=\"arbox k-update\"><h5>Current evidence</h5><p><b>Food bolus.</b> Most impactions pass; endoscopy within 24 hours (sooner if the patient cannot swallow saliva), and do not use carbonated drinks or glucagon in place of it.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-6\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.6</span><h3>Oesophagoscopy</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b3-6-1\"><h4><i>3.6.1</i>Rigid oesophagoscopy</h4><p>Rigid oesophagoscopy is still the instrument for sharp or impacted foreign bodies, for biopsy of cricopharyngeal and upper oesophageal lesions, for dilatation, for endoscopic pouch surgery and for panendoscopy in head and neck cancer. It is contraindicated by cervical spine disease or injury, trismus, a receding jaw, aortic aneurysm and severe systemic illness.</p><ol class=\"arol\"><li>General anaesthesia with the tube to the left of the mouth. Supine with the neck flexed and the head extended (the \"sniffing\" position), so the mouth, pharynx and oesophagus align. Protect the upper teeth.</li><li>Pass the scope down the right side of the tongue to the arytenoids, then behind the cricoid in the midline.</li><li>Open the cricopharyngeus by <em class=\"term\">gentle, sustained lifting</em>, never force. This is where perforation happens.</li><li>Keep the lumen in view at all times. Aortic pulsation marks the 25 cm level. The red, velvety gastric mucosa marks the cardia.</li><li>Inspect again on withdrawal.</li></ol><p>The complications are injury to the teeth, lips and arytenoids, tracheal compression in children, and <em class=\"term\">perforation</em>, which is why pain, fever and emphysema after the procedure are taken seriously.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b3-6-2\"><h4><i>3.6.2</i>Flexible and transnasal oesophagoscopy</h4><p><b>Flexible oesophagoscopy</b> is done under topical anaesthesia or sedation in the left lateral position through a mouth guard. It sees the oesophagus, stomach and duodenum; allows biopsy, dilatation, banding, stents and removal of soft foreign bodies; is safe in neck and jaw disease; and has largely replaced the rigid scope for diagnosis. <b>Transnasal oesophagoscopy</b> is an unsedated office examination with a thin scope passed through the nose, used for dysphagia, reflux and Barrett's screening, the search for a second primary, and tracheo-oesophageal puncture after laryngectomy.</p><figure class=\"arfig\" id=\"a-f6\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-arfig=\"d93-1\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 6\"><img src=\"clinic/fig/d93-1.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 6</b>Flexible oesophagoscopy in the left lateral position, through a mouth guard, viewing on a monitor.<cite>Dhingra, fig. 93.1</cite></figcaption></figure><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><caption>Rigid versus flexible oesophagoscopy</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Feature</th><th scope=\"col\">Rigid</th><th scope=\"col\">Flexible</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Anaesthesia</td><td>General</td><td>Topical with or without sedation</td></tr><tr><td>Neck and jaw disease</td><td>Difficult or contraindicated</td><td>Possible</td></tr><tr><td>Reach</td><td>Oesophagus</td><td>Oesophagus, stomach, duodenum</td></tr><tr><td>Foreign bodies</td><td>Best for sharp and impacted objects; can sheath sharp points</td><td>Soft, blunt objects; needs an overtube for sharp ones</td></tr><tr><td>Biopsy of the upper sphincter region</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Can miss the postcricoid area</td></tr><tr><td>Setting</td><td>Inpatient theatre</td><td>Day case or office</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-7\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.7</span><h3>Investigations: what and when</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><caption>Dysphagia: investigations</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Role</th><th scope=\"col\">Test</th><th scope=\"col\">When and what it shows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Red flags (age 55 and over with weight loss, any progressive dysphagia)</td><td>Urgent direct-access upper GI endoscopy with biopsy (NICE NG12)</td><td>Cancer, stricture, eosinophilic oesophagitis</td></tr><tr><td>Oropharyngeal dysphagia</td><td>Flexible endoscopic evaluation of swallowing (FEES) or videofluoroscopy with speech and language therapy</td><td>Aspiration, residue, pouch, cricopharyngeal bar</td></tr><tr><td>Pouch, web, ring, motility pattern</td><td>Barium swallow</td><td>Anatomy and transit</td></tr><tr><td>Motility (gold standard)</td><td>High-resolution manometry</td><td>Achalasia subtypes, spastic disorders</td></tr><tr><td>Reflux not responding to PPI, before surgery</td><td>24-hour pH–impedance</td><td>Acid and non-acid reflux</td></tr><tr><td>Cancer staging</td><td>CT chest and abdomen, PET-CT, endoscopic ultrasound</td><td>Stage</td></tr><tr><td>Suspected perforation</td><td>Water-soluble contrast swallow and CT</td><td>Leak</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c4\" data-t=\"Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Heartburn, and the larynx.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s4-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">4.1</span><h3>Etiology</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Reflux disease is a failure of the barrier, through weakness or transient relaxation of the lower sphincter, hiatus hernia, obesity, pregnancy, smoking, alcohol, fatty meals, scleroderma, and drugs that relax smooth muscle such as calcium-channel blockers, nitrates and anticholinergics.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s4-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">4.2</span><h3>Clinical features</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Its typical symptoms are heartburn and regurgitation. For the ENT surgeon the extra-oesophageal effects matter as much. <em class=\"term\">Laryngopharyngeal reflux</em> (<button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-laryngitis\">Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections</button>) causes hoarseness, throat clearing, globus and chronic cough, with posterior laryngitis, contact ulcers and granulomas; reflux also contributes to subglottic stenosis, asthma, dental erosion and, in children, otitis media with effusion.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s4-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">4.3</span><h3>Management</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Treatment starts with lifestyle change: weight loss, raising the head of the bed, no meals in the three hours before bed, stopping smoking, and less alcohol, caffeine, chocolate and fat. Proton pump inhibitors, H2 blockers and alginates follow, and antireflux surgery such as a <em class=\"term\">Nissen fundoplication</em> is reserved for refractory disease.</p><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b4-3-1\"><h4><i>4.3.1</i>Drugs and doses</h4><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><caption>Reflux (GORD and laryngopharyngeal reflux): drugs and doses</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Step</th><th scope=\"col\">Drug and dose</th><th scope=\"col\">Course and notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Every patient</td><td>Weight loss, head of bed raised, no food 3 hours before bed, stop smoking, less alcohol</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>GORD, first line</td><td>Full-dose PPI: omeprazole 20 mg or lansoprazole 30 mg once daily before breakfast</td><td>4 weeks (8 weeks for severe oesophagitis); then lowest dose that controls symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>GORD, not responding</td><td>Double-dose PPI or add an H2 blocker at night (famotidine 20 to 40 mg)</td><td>Endoscopy if persistent</td></tr><tr><td>Laryngopharyngeal reflux</td><td>Alginate (for example sodium alginate–potassium bicarbonate 10 ml after meals and at bedtime); PPI trial (omeprazole 20 to 40 mg twice daily) only with typical reflux symptoms</td><td>8 to 12 weeks, then stop if no benefit; evidence for PPIs in LPR is weak</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Doses from NICE CG184 (Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease and dyspepsia in adults) and the BNF; LPR approach from ENT UK guidance and trials of alginate and PPI.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s4-4\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">4.4</span><h3>Complications</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The complications are erosive oesophagitis, peptic stricture and <em class=\"term\">Barrett's oesophagus</em>, in which columnar <em class=\"term\">intestinal metaplasia</em> replaces the squamous lining of the distal oesophagus. It appears as salmon-pink mucosa, is premalignant for <em class=\"term\">adenocarcinoma</em>, and needs endoscopic surveillance.</p><div class=\"arbox k-update\"><h5>Current evidence</h5><p><b>Barrett's oesophagus.</b> The British Society of Gastroenterology guideline recommends endoscopic surveillance according to length and dysplasia, and endoscopic resection and ablation for dysplasia (Fitzgerald et al., <em class=\"term\">Gut</em> 2014).</p></div></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c5\" data-t=\"Infective oesophagitis\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Infective oesophagitis</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Odynophagia in the immunosuppressed.</p><p>Infection occurs in the immunosuppressed, after chemotherapy or steroids, in HIV and in diabetes, and presents with odynophagia. <b>Candida</b> produces white plaques and is treated with fluconazole; <b>herpes simplex</b> produces small punched-out ulcers and is treated with aciclovir; and <b>cytomegalovirus</b> produces large shallow ulcers and is treated with ganciclovir.</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c6\" data-t=\"Eosinophilic oesophagitis\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Eosinophilic oesophagitis</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Young atopic men and bolus impaction.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s6-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">6.1</span><h3>Clinical features</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Eosinophilic oesophagitis typically affects young atopic men with asthma, eczema or food allergy, and presents with dysphagia and <em class=\"term\">recurrent food bolus impaction</em>.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s6-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">6.2</span><h3>Diagnosis</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Endoscopy shows rings (\"trachealisation\"), furrows and white exudates, and biopsy shows <em class=\"term\">15 or more eosinophils per high-power field</em>.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s6-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">6.3</span><h3>Management</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>It is treated with a PPI, swallowed topical steroids such as budesonide or fluticasone, elimination diets and careful dilatation.</p><div class=\"arbox k-update\"><h5>Current evidence</h5><p><b>Eosinophilic oesophagitis.</b> Biopsies are taken from at least two levels; budesonide orodispersible tablets are a swallowed steroid option, and dupilumab improved histology and dysphagia in a phase 3 trial (Dellon et al., <em class=\"term\">NEJM</em> 2022).</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b6-3-1\"><h4><i>6.3.1</i>Drugs and doses</h4><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><caption>Eosinophilic oesophagitis: drugs and doses</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Step</th><th scope=\"col\">Drug and dose</th><th scope=\"col\">Course and notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Option 1</td><td>High-dose PPI: omeprazole 20 to 40 mg twice a day</td><td>8 to 12 weeks, then repeat biopsies</td></tr><tr><td>Option 2</td><td>Budesonide orodispersible tablet 1 mg twice a day</td><td>6 weeks (up to 12), then maintenance 0.5 to 1 mg twice a day</td></tr><tr><td>Option 3</td><td>Elimination diet (empirical 2- to 6-food) with a dietitian</td><td>Re-scope after each step</td></tr><tr><td>Strictures</td><td>Endoscopic dilatation</td><td>With medical treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Refractory (specialist)</td><td>Dupilumab 300 mg SC weekly (licensed for eosinophilic oesophagitis)</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Based on the BSG guideline on eosinophilic oesophagitis (Dhar et al., <em class=\"term\">Gut</em> 2022) and NICE TA708 (budesonide orodispersible tablet).</p></div></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c7\" data-t=\"Pill and radiation oesophagitis\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Pill and radiation oesophagitis</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">The aortic narrowing.</p><p>Tetracyclines and doxycycline, potassium chloride, iron, NSAIDs and bisphosphonates can lodge at the aortic narrowing and ulcerate it, especially when taken at bedtime without water. Radiation causes acute ulceration and, later, stricture and dysmotility.</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c8\" data-t=\"Caustic injury\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Caustic injury</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Alkali and acid.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s8-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">8.1</span><h3>Etiology</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Two patterns are seen: a toddler who swallows a household product by accident, usually in small amounts with less severe injury, and an adolescent or adult who swallows a large amount on purpose, with severe injury. <b>Alkalis</b> such as drain cleaner and lye cause <em class=\"term\">liquefactive necrosis</em> and penetrate deeply into the oesophagus. <b>Acids</b> cause <em class=\"term\">coagulative necrosis</em>, which limits their penetration, and damage the stomach more, leading to pyloric stenosis or gastric perforation.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s8-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">8.2</span><h3>Pathophysiology</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The injury evolves in four stages:</p><ol class=\"arol\"><li>Acute necrosis in the first days.</li><li>Sloughing and granulation over two to three weeks, when the wall is weakest and perforation most likely.</li><li>Fibrosis and <em class=\"term\">stricture</em> from about the third week, over months.</li><li>A lifelong increased risk of <em class=\"term\">squamous carcinoma</em>, decades later.</li></ol></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s8-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">8.3</span><h3>Diagnosis</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Assessment begins with the airway: stridor, hoarseness and drooling mean laryngeal injury, which may need intubation or tracheostomy. Burns of the lips and mouth are unreliable, because their absence does not exclude oesophageal injury. Look for perforation, mediastinitis or peritonitis, and take chest and abdominal films, with CT if perforation is suspected. Endoscopy is then done gently within 12 to 48 hours, going no further than a severe circumferential burn, and graded by Zargar's system: grade 0 normal, 1 oedema and erythema, 2a superficial ulcers and exudates, 2b deep or circumferential ulcers, 3a focal necrosis and 3b extensive necrosis. Grades 0 to 2a heal without stricture; grade 2b and above often stricture.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s8-4\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">8.4</span><h3>Management</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Management follows a sequence:</p><ol class=\"arol\"><li>Resuscitate and protect the airway.</li><li><em class=\"term\">No induced vomiting, no neutralising agents and no blind nasogastric tube</em> (most current guidance).</li><li>Analgesia and acid suppression.</li><li>Nil by mouth at first; a nasogastric tube placed under vision, or a gastrostomy, for feeding in deep burns.</li><li>Antibiotics for deep burns or perforation.</li><li>Steroids are controversial and not used routinely.</li><li>Emergency oesophagectomy or gastrectomy for full-thickness necrosis or perforation.</li><li>Follow-up contrast studies for stricture.</li></ol><div class=\"arbox k-differ\"><h5>Where the books differ · Corrosive burns: neutralisation and steroids</h5><div class=\"cols\"><div><b>Dhingra</b>Neutralisation with a weak acid or alkali within six hours, a nasogastric tube to keep the lumen open, and steroids from 48 to 96 hours for four to six weeks to prevent stricture.</div><div><b>Lee</b>Supportive care, early endoscopic grading, airway protection when the larynx is involved, and management of later strictures, with no routine neutralisation or steroids.</div><div><b>In practice</b>Avoid neutralisation, because the heat of the reaction adds injury and vomiting re-exposes the oesophagus. Do not give steroids routinely, because trials have not shown that they prevent stricture.</div></div></div><div class=\"arbox k-update\"><h5>Current evidence</h5><p><b>Caustic ingestion.</b> Endoscopy within 12 to 48 hours grades the injury (Zargar classification) and predicts stricture; CT is increasingly used to decide who needs surgery.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b8-4-1\"><h4><i>8.4.1</i>Drugs and doses</h4><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><caption>Caustic injury: drugs and doses</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Step</th><th scope=\"col\">Treatment</th><th scope=\"col\">Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Never</td><td>Induced vomiting, neutralisation, or nasogastric tube passed blind</td><td>Re-exposes the oesophagus</td></tr><tr><td>Airway</td><td>Early airway assessment; intubation or tracheostomy for laryngeal burns</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Every patient</td><td>Nil by mouth, IV fluids, analgesia, IV PPI (for example omeprazole 40 mg daily)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Endoscopy (within 12 to 48 hours) or CT</td><td>Grades the injury</td><td>Avoid days 5 to 15 (weakest wall)</td></tr><tr><td>Perforation or full-thickness necrosis</td><td>Broad-spectrum antibiotics and surgery</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Not recommended</td><td>Routine corticosteroids to prevent stricture</td><td>No proven benefit</td></tr><tr><td>Stricture</td><td>Serial dilatation from about 3 weeks</td><td>Lifelong cancer risk</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s8-5\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">8.5</span><h3>Complications</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Strictures are treated by repeated dilatation, retrograde dilatation through a gastrostomy over a swallowed string when the stricture cannot be passed from above, and oesophageal replacement with colon or stomach when dilatation fails.</p></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c9\" data-t=\"Perforation\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Perforation</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Instrumentation, and Boerhaave.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s9-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">9.1</span><h3>Etiology</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Instrumentation is the commonest cause of perforation: rigid oesophagoscopy, dilatation and the removal of foreign bodies, usually just above or at the cricopharyngeus at Killian's dehiscence, or in the distal oesophagus. Foreign bodies, trauma and malignancy are other causes, and <em class=\"term\">Boerhaave syndrome</em> is a spontaneous full-thickness rupture after vomiting, usually of the left lower oesophagus.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s9-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">9.2</span><h3>Clinical features</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>A <b>cervical perforation</b> causes neck pain, dysphagia, fever, tenderness and <em class=\"term\">surgical emphysema</em> in the neck. A <b>thoracic perforation</b> causes severe chest or <em class=\"term\">interscapular</em> pain, fever, tachycardia and shock, with surgical emphysema, <em class=\"term\">Hamman's sign</em> (a mediastinal crunch with each heartbeat), pneumothorax and pleural effusion. <em class=\"term\">Mackler's triad</em> of vomiting, chest pain and subcutaneous emphysema is the classic picture of Boerhaave syndrome.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s9-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">9.3</span><h3>Differential diagnosis</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Boerhaave syndrome must be distinguished from the <b>Mallory–Weiss</b> tear, a mucosal tear at the gastro-oesophageal junction after retching that presents with haematemesis and is not a perforation.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s9-4\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">9.4</span><h3>Diagnosis</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Neck and chest films show air in the prevertebral space, mediastinum or pleura, and a <em class=\"term\">water-soluble contrast swallow</em> and CT confirm the leak.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s9-5\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">9.5</span><h3>Management</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Every patient is kept nil by mouth on intravenous fluids, broad-spectrum antibiotics and antifungals, with nutritional support. Small contained cervical perforations are often managed conservatively, with cervical drainage if an abscess forms. Thoracic perforations need early surgical repair, ideally within about 24 hours, with drainage, or endoscopic stents and clips in selected cases; late perforations need drainage, diversion or resection.</p><div class=\"arbox k-flag\"><h5>Pain after an endoscopy</h5><p>Neck, chest or back pain, fever, tachycardia or crepitus after oesophagoscopy or dilatation means perforation until proved otherwise. Stop oral intake, image with water-soluble contrast and CT, and start antibiotics. Every hour of delay raises mortality.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b9-5-1\"><h4><i>9.5.1</i>Drugs and doses</h4><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><caption>Oesophageal perforation: drugs and doses (adults)</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Step</th><th scope=\"col\">Drug and dose</th><th scope=\"col\">Course and notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Every patient</td><td>Nil by mouth, IV fluids, nutrition (jejunal or parenteral)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Antibiotic</td><td>Piperacillin–tazobactam 4.5 g IV every 8 hours (or per local policy)</td><td>Oral flora and Gram-negatives</td></tr><tr><td>Antifungal</td><td>Fluconazole 400 mg IV daily (higher-risk patients)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Acid</td><td>IV PPI</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Source control</td><td>Conservative for small contained cervical leaks; stent, drainage or surgery for others</td><td>Surgical team</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Doses from the BNF; regimens follow published management reviews and local policy.</p></div></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c10\" data-t=\"Achalasia\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Achalasia</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">The bird's beak.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s10-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">10.1</span><h3>Etiology and pathophysiology</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Achalasia results from loss of the inhibitory neurons of the <em class=\"term\">myenteric (Auerbach's) plexus</em>, producing <em class=\"term\">aperistalsis</em> and failure of the lower sphincter to relax, usually with a high resting pressure. A secondary form occurs in <em class=\"term\">Chagas disease</em> from <em class=\"term\">Trypanosoma cruzi</em>.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s10-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">10.2</span><h3>Clinical features</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The patient has dysphagia to <em class=\"term\">both solids and liquids</em> from the outset, regurgitates undigested food especially at night with cough and aspiration, has chest pain, and loses weight slowly.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s10-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">10.3</span><h3>Diagnosis</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The barium swallow shows a dilated oesophagus with a fluid level tapering smoothly to a \"<em class=\"term\">bird's beak</em>\" at the cardia, and a chest film may show a widened mediastinum and an absent gastric bubble. Manometry is the gold standard, and the Chicago classification divides achalasia into types I to III, which predicts the response to treatment.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s10-4\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">10.4</span><h3>Differential diagnosis</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p><em class=\"term\">Pseudoachalasia</em> is produced by a cancer at the cardia, which must always be excluded by endoscopy, especially in an older patient with rapid weight loss.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s10-5\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">10.5</span><h3>Management</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The options are <em class=\"term\">pneumatic dilatation</em>, laparoscopic <em class=\"term\">Heller myotomy</em> with a partial fundoplication, and <em class=\"term\">peroral endoscopic myotomy (POEM)</em>. Botulinum toxin injected into the sphincter gives temporary relief in the frail, and calcium-channel blockers and nitrates have only a weak effect.</p><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b10-5-1\"><h4><i>10.5.1</i>Treatment: what and when</h4><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><caption>Achalasia: treatment</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Patient</th><th scope=\"col\">Treatment and dose</th><th scope=\"col\">Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fit for intervention (first line)</td><td>Pneumatic dilatation, laparoscopic Heller myotomy with partial fundoplication, or POEM</td><td>Choice by subtype (POEM favoured in type III) and local expertise</td></tr><tr><td>Frail or unfit</td><td>Botulinum toxin 100 units injected into the lower sphincter at endoscopy</td><td>Relief for months; repeat</td></tr><tr><td>Bridging only</td><td>Nifedipine 10 to 30 mg sublingually 30 to 60 minutes before meals, or isosorbide dinitrate</td><td>Limited benefit, side effects</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Based on the UEG and ESNM achalasia guideline (Oude Nijhuis et al., <em class=\"term\">United European Gastroenterol J</em> 2020).</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s10-6\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">10.6</span><h3>Complications</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The long-term risk of squamous carcinoma is increased.</p></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c11\" data-t=\"Spastic disorders\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Spastic disorders</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Corkscrew and nutcracker.</p><p><b>Distal (diffuse) oesophageal spasm</b> produces premature, non-propulsive contractions with normal relaxation of the lower sphincter. It causes intermittent dysphagia and chest pain that mimics angina, and the barium swallow shows a \"<em class=\"term\">corkscrew</em>\" or \"<em class=\"term\">rosary bead</em>\" oesophagus. It is treated with nitrates, calcium-channel blockers, botulinum toxin and sometimes myotomy. The <b>hypercontractile</b> (\"nutcracker\" or \"jackhammer\") oesophagus has very strong contractions that remain peristaltic, and causes chest pain.</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c12\" data-t=\"Systemic causes of dysmotility\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Systemic causes of dysmotility</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Scleroderma and others.</p><p>In <b>scleroderma</b> (systemic sclerosis), smooth-muscle atrophy and fibrosis give <em class=\"term\">aperistalsis of the lower two-thirds</em> and a <em class=\"term\">weak lower sphincter</em>, with severe reflux, strictures and Barrett's oesophagus, as part of the CREST syndrome. Polymyositis and dermatomyositis affect the striated upper oesophagus, and myasthenia gravis, motor neurone disease, diabetes, hypothyroidism and presbyoesophagus also disturb motility.</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c13\" data-t=\"Cricopharyngeal dysfunction\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Cricopharyngeal dysfunction</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">The upper sphincter that will not open.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s13-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">13.1</span><h3>Etiology</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Here the upper sphincter fails to relax or open, from fibrosis of the muscle, neurological disease such as stroke, Parkinson disease, motor neurone disease or muscular dystrophy, or reflux.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s13-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">13.2</span><h3>Diagnosis</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Videofluoroscopy shows a posterior <em class=\"term\">cricopharyngeal bar</em>, though a bar is also seen in up to a third of asymptomatic elderly people, so it must be interpreted with the symptoms.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s13-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">13.3</span><h3>Management</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>It is treated by dilatation, botulinum toxin injection or <em class=\"term\">cricopharyngeal myotomy</em>, endoscopic or external; myotomy is avoided in severe reflux.</p><div class=\"arops\" data-arops=\"46 45\" data-lead=\"Dysfunction of the upper oesophageal sphincter, and oesophagoscopy and hypopharyngoscopy.\"></div></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c14\" data-t=\"Plummer–Vinson syndrome\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Plummer–Vinson syndrome</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Paterson–Brown-Kelly.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s14-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">14.1</span><h3>Clinical features</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>This syndrome affects middle-aged women and combines iron-deficiency anaemia with dysphagia from a <em class=\"term\">postcricoid web</em>, glossitis, angular stomatitis, koilonychia, atrophic mucosa and sometimes achlorhydria.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s14-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">14.2</span><h3>Management</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>It is treated with iron, with B12 and B6 if deficient, dilatation of the web, and surveillance.</p><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b14-2-1\"><h4><i>14.2.1</i>Drugs and doses</h4><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><caption>Plummer–Vinson syndrome: drugs and doses</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Step</th><th scope=\"col\">Drug and dose</th><th scope=\"col\">Course and notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Iron deficiency</td><td>Ferrous sulfate 200 mg (65 mg iron) once daily, or on alternate days if not tolerated</td><td>3 months after haemoglobin normalises; IV iron if oral fails</td></tr><tr><td>Web</td><td>Endoscopic dilatation</td><td>Often relieves dysphagia at once</td></tr><tr><td>Surveillance</td><td>Endoscopy for postcricoid carcinoma</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Iron dosing from the BSG iron deficiency anaemia guideline (Snook et al., <em class=\"term\">Gut</em> 2021).</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s14-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">14.3</span><h3>Complications</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>It carries an increased risk of <em class=\"term\">postcricoid</em> (<button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-hypoca\">Carcinoma of the hypopharynx</button>) and oesophageal carcinoma, about 10% in the older literature.</p></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c15\" data-t=\"Schatzki ring\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Schatzki ring</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Steakhouse syndrome.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s15-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">15.1</span><h3>Clinical features</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>A Schatzki ring is a thin mucosal ring at the squamocolumnar junction, often with a small hiatus hernia, in people over 50. It causes intermittent dysphagia to solids and, once the lumen is under 13 mm, the \"steakhouse syndrome\" of bolus impaction.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s15-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">15.2</span><h3>Management</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>It is treated by dilatation and a PPI.</p></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c16\" data-t=\"Diverticula\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Diverticula</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Pulsion and traction.</p><p>The <b>pharyngeal (Zenker's) pouch</b> is a pulsion diverticulum through Killian's dehiscence and is covered in <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-pouch\">Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch</button>. A <b>mid-oesophageal traction diverticulum</b> is pulled out by inflamed mediastinal nodes, classically tuberculous, and an <b>epiphrenic diverticulum</b> is a pulsion diverticulum just above the lower sphincter, usually secondary to a motility disorder.</p><div class=\"arcases\" data-arcases=\"zenker-diverticulum-1\" data-lead=\"A pharyngeal (Zenker) diverticulum on barium swallow.\"></div></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c17\" data-t=\"Hiatus hernia\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Hiatus hernia</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Sliding and rolling.</p><p>In the <b>sliding</b> hernia, about 90% of the total, the gastro-oesophageal junction slides up into the chest, and reflux is common. In the <b>para-oesophageal (rolling)</b> hernia the junction stays below the diaphragm while the fundus rolls up beside it; reflux is slight, but the hernia can bleed, obstruct or strangulate, and repair is recommended.</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c18\" data-t=\"Strictures\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Strictures</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Biopsy every one.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s18-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">18.1</span><h3>Etiology</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Benign strictures may be peptic, caustic, radiation-induced, anastomotic after surgery, pill-induced, the result of a foreign body, eosinophilic or congenital.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s18-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">18.2</span><h3>Management</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Every stricture is biopsied to exclude cancer. Treatment is dilatation with bougies, often over a guidewire, or balloons, together with treatment of the cause; stents or surgery are reserved for refractory cases.</p></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c19\" data-t=\"Congenital anomalies\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Congenital anomalies</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Atresia, fistula and vascular rings.</p><p><b>Oesophageal atresia with tracheo-oesophageal fistula</b> is commonest (about 85%) as <em class=\"term\">proximal atresia with a distal fistula</em>. The baby drools, chokes and becomes cyanosed at the first feed, the abdomen distends, and a nasogastric tube coils in the upper pouch at 9 to 13 cm. It is associated with the <em class=\"term\">VACTERL</em> anomalies. The <b>H-type fistula</b> (about 4%) presents later with coughing on feeds and recurrent pneumonia. <b>Vascular rings and an aberrant right subclavian artery</b> cause dysphagia lusoria, and the latter brings a non-recurrent right laryngeal nerve that is at risk in thyroid surgery.</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c20\" data-t=\"Benign tumours\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Benign tumours</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Leiomyoma.</p><p>The <b>leiomyoma</b> accounts for about two-thirds of benign tumours. It is a smooth, intramural submucosal mass that causes dysphagia only when large and is best seen with endoscopic ultrasound; mucosal biopsy is avoided, because it scars the plane needed for enucleation. <b>Fibrovascular polyps</b> of the upper oesophagus can be regurgitated into the mouth and obstruct the larynx, and papillomas, lipomas, haemangiomas and granular cell tumours also occur.</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc amdz\" id=\"a-c21\" data-t=\"Oesophageal cancer\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2><small>Disease</small>Oesophageal cancer</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Progressive dysphagia and weight loss.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s21-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">21.1</span><h3>Epidemiology and etiology</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p><b>Squamous cell carcinoma</b> is the commonest type worldwide and in India, and arises mostly in the <em class=\"term\">middle and upper</em> oesophagus. Its risk factors are tobacco, smoked and chewed, and alcohol; very hot drinks; dietary nitrosamines and poor diet; <em class=\"term\">achalasia</em>; <em class=\"term\">caustic strictures</em>; Plummer–Vinson syndrome; tylosis (palmoplantar keratoderma); and a previous head and neck cancer through field change. <b>Adenocarcinoma</b> is the commonest type in Western countries, arises in the <em class=\"term\">distal</em> oesophagus and cardia, and develops in <em class=\"term\">Barrett's oesophagus</em> with reflux and obesity.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s21-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">21.2</span><h3>Pathophysiology: spread</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The tumour spreads directly into the trachea, bronchi, aorta, pericardium and recurrent laryngeal nerves; through lymphatics to cervical, mediastinal and coeliac nodes, including skip lesions; and through the blood to liver, lung and bone.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s21-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">21.3</span><h3>Clinical features</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The cardinal symptoms are <em class=\"term\">progressive dysphagia, first to solids and then to liquids</em>, and <em class=\"term\">weight loss</em>. Odynophagia and back pain suggest spread outside the wall, hoarseness means recurrent nerve involvement, and coughing on swallowing means a <em class=\"term\">tracheo-oesophageal fistula</em>. A sudden bolus obstruction may be the first sign.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s21-4\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">21.4</span><h3>Diagnosis</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Diagnosis is by endoscopy and biopsy. The barium swallow shows an irregular \"<em class=\"term\">apple-core</em>\" narrowing with shouldering, and staging uses CT of chest and abdomen, <em class=\"term\">PET-CT</em> and endoscopic ultrasound for depth and nodes, with bronchoscopy for tumours near the airway.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s21-5\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">21.5</span><h3>Management</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Early tumours are resected endoscopically. Resectable locally advanced disease is treated with neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy (the CROSS regimen) or perioperative chemotherapy followed by oesophagectomy; definitive chemoradiotherapy is a standard option for squamous carcinoma, particularly in the upper and cervical oesophagus. The stomach is brought up as a gastric pull-up to reconstruct, while the cervical oesophagus after pharyngo-oesophagectomy is replaced with a free jejunal flap or a tubed radial forearm or anterolateral thigh flap. <b>Cervical oesophageal cancer</b> is often an extension of postcricoid or hypopharyngeal cancer and may need a <em class=\"term\">pharyngolaryngo-oesophagectomy</em> with gastric pull-up. For palliation, self-expanding metal <em class=\"term\">stents</em>, brachytherapy, laser or argon plasma ablation and a feeding gastrostomy or jejunostomy relieve dysphagia, and a covered stent seals a tracheo-oesophageal fistula.</p><div class=\"arops\" data-arops=\"50\" data-lead=\"Cancer of the cervical oesophagus.\"></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s21-6\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">21.6</span><h3>Prognosis</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The prognosis is poor: overall five-year survival is about 15 to 20%, because more than half of patients have unresectable or metastatic disease at diagnosis.</p></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc\" id=\"a-c22\" data-t=\"Tips and links\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2>Tips and links</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">For the examination and the ward.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s22-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">22.1</span><h3>Viva essentials</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbox k-exam\"><h5>What the examiner is looking for</h5><ul><li><b>The three constrictions</b> and their distances; Killian dehiscence.</li><li><b>The three phases of swallowing</b> and the role of laryngeal elevation.</li><li><b>Oropharyngeal versus oesophageal dysphagia;</b> solids versus liquids; the investigation sequence.</li><li><b>Achalasia:</b> bird's beak, manometry, pseudoachalasia; dilatation, Heller myotomy, POEM.</li><li><b>Plummer–Vinson syndrome</b> and postcricoid cancer.</li><li><b>Caustic injury:</b> alkali versus acid, the stages, grading, and the stricture and cancer risk.</li><li><b>Perforation:</b> causes and signs; Boerhaave syndrome.</li><li><b>Barrett oesophagus</b> and adenocarcinoma; risk factors for squamous carcinoma.</li><li><b>Rigid oesophagoscopy:</b> position, steps, complications.</li></ul></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s22-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">22.2</span><h3>Common pitfalls</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbox k-pit\"><h5>Common pitfalls</h5><ul><li>Calling progressive dysphagia in an older patient \"globus\" without endoscopy.</li><li>Forcing a rigid scope through the cricopharyngeus.</li><li>Missing perforation in a patient with pain after endoscopy.</li><li>Treating achalasia without excluding a cancer at the cardia.</li><li>Inducing vomiting or neutralising after a caustic ingestion.</li></ul></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s22-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">22.3</span><h3>Related topics</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><ul class=\"arl\"><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-pouch\">Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch</button>, <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-fbfood\">Swallowed foreign bodies</button>, <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-hypoca\">Carcinoma of the hypopharynx</button>, <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-laryngitis\">Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections</button>, <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-pharanat\">Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx</button>, <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-ap_dysph\">Approach to difficulty swallowing</button></li></ul></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc\" id=\"a-c23\" data-t=\"Sources\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2>Sources</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Where this topic comes from.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s23-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">23.1</span><h3>Books</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><ul class=\"arl\"><li>Phillips and Bhalla, <em class=\"term\">Oxford Textbook of Otolaryngology</em> (2025), chapter 48.</li><li><em class=\"term\">Oxford Specialist Handbook of Otolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery</em>, 2nd edition, chapter 10.</li><li><em class=\"term\">Oxford Handbook of ENT and Head and Neck Surgery</em>, 3rd edition (2020), chapter 13.</li><li>Dhingra, <em class=\"term\">Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat</em>, chapters 67, 68, 69 and 93.</li><li>K. J. Lee, <em class=\"term\">Essential Otolaryngology</em>, chapters 36 and 45.</li></ul></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s23-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">23.2</span><h3>Studies cited</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><ul class=\"arl\"><li>Dellon et al., <em class=\"term\">N Engl J Med</em> 2022: dupilumab for eosinophilic oesophagitis.</li><li>Fitzgerald et al., <em class=\"term\">Gut</em> 2014: British Society of Gastroenterology guideline on Barrett's oesophagus.</li></ul></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s23-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">23.3</span><h3>Guidelines and dosing</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><ul class=\"arl\"><li>NICE CG184: Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease and dyspepsia in adults.</li><li>Dhar et al., <em class=\"term\">Gut</em> 2022: BSG guideline on eosinophilic oesophagitis.</li><li>NICE TA708: Budesonide orodispersible tablet for eosinophilic oesophagitis.</li><li>Oude Nijhuis et al., <em class=\"term\">United European Gastroenterol J</em> 2020: European guideline on achalasia.</li><li>Snook et al., <em class=\"term\">Gut</em> 2021: BSG guideline on iron deficiency anaemia.</li><li>BNF: omeprazole, lansoprazole, famotidine, sodium alginate, budesonide, ferrous sulfate, botulinum toxin.</li></ul></div></details></div></details></section>", "stand": "Dysphagia is a symptom with a long list of causes, from a stroke to a stricture to a cancer. Most are sorted by asking three questions. Is the problem getting food out of the mouth and pharynx (oropharyngeal), or getting it down the oesophagus (oesophageal)? Is it worse for solids or for liquids? Is it progressive or intermittent? Progressive dysphagia to solids, then liquids, with weight loss in an older smoker is cancer until endoscopy proves otherwise.", "ch": ["Summary", "Applied anatomy", "Diagnosis", "Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease", "Infective oesophagitis", "Eosinophilic oesophagitis", "Pill and radiation oesophagitis", "Caustic injury", "Perforation", "Achalasia", "Spastic disorders", "Systemic causes of dysmotility", "Cricopharyngeal dysfunction", "Plummer–Vinson syndrome", "Schatzki ring", "Diverticula", "Hiatus hernia", "Strictures", "Congenital anomalies", "Benign tumours", "Oesophageal cancer", "Tips and links", "Sources"], "w": 5316, "n": 56, "lay": "amboss"};
